{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7C59nUBBBHN6GbEuRi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017vJm2uM9XUaYAyKXYbJVKo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='data/about.json'\na=json.load(open(p))\na['sourceInformation']['online']='AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2-4 players (host runs the bouts; checked with the hub harness); local versus 1-2 players on one keyboard'\na['sourceInformation']['testing']='Headless Node suites on the engine (54 checks: data, technique, bout, AI, bonus, flow, story playthrough, dev), browser screenshots, the AI Hub harness (4 configurations)'\na['sourceInformation']['research']='Brief: the facts in PLAN.md section 1.1 (scoring, timer, bonus stages as summarized there); no other research'\na['sourceInformation']['usage']='unknown'\na['testingFeedback']=[\n \"About 12 issues were caught by the tests, screenshots and playthrough runs. Most were logic and wiring faults (a missing alias that broke boot, a dev scene field overwritten, a drill that matched the wrong technique, a fighter with no personality).\",\n \"Four were breaking: the boot failure, the practice scene crash, the dev screen crash, and the store that kept no save headless, which stopped the story flow. All four are fixed and the suite is green.\",\n \"The most helpful test was the scripted story playthrough over the whole ladder, because it exercised every scene transition at once.\",\n \"The least helpful was the 20-seed CPU balance probe: it mostly showed the random stream dominating outcomes, and it was hard to read without the side and seed swap.\"\n]\na['engineFeedback']=[\n \"The Humanoid rig and E.Backdrop, with the Dialog and Menu helpers, let a complete fight look right with no art files. The headless game.advance plus draw option made scene checks possible.\",\n \"Stuck or guessed wrong: game.go is a fade, so a state read right after it is one second early; the store reports false with no storage. Both cost time before I knew to wait or add a memory fallback.\",\n \"Built myself: the fight simulation (technique matrix, timing, scoring, blocks, aiuchi, hantei), the CPU, the bonus rules, the drills, the online Kachinuki and the gibberish voice synth.\",\n \"Hardest part: getting the pose from fight state into the rig each step without drawing on every frame, and keeping the rig state serializable for guests.\",\n \"Engine changes: none. The game uses the engine as shipped and works around its gaps in game code.\",\n \"One thing to change: a headless store that keeps data in memory, so Node tests can use the same save path.\"\n]\na['promptFeedback']=[\n \"The staged structure (read STAGE.md, then TASK.md wins over PLAN.md) and the explicit done-when list made the deliverables clear.\",\n \"Confusing: the plan's file names still say opus-max, while TASK.md says haiku-medium; I used TASK.md's names. Also the plan describes far more scope than one session can finish.\",\n \"Hardest: the online netcode and the test harness wiring, which both need exact protocol details.\",\n \"Change: state the scope cut-off explicitly (for example which features are must-have for a first build) and the expected test counts.\"\n]\na['sourceInformation']['usage']='unknown'\na['aiHub']['players']='1–4'\na['aiHub']['controls']='Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause'\njson.dump(a,open(p,'w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('written')\nEOF\nnode tools/build.mjs | tail -1 && node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('../exit-interview.json','utf8')); console.log('exit json ok')\"","description":"Write feedback answers into about.json and rebuild the bundle"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1931,"cache_read_input_tokens":416311,"cache_crea