p1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315.html` (about 1.4 MB, the engine and the AI Hub client inlined, no external requests).\n- `exit-interview.json` in the same folder; it parses and is generated from `data/about.json`, which the About screen also reads.\n- Dev folder: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315/` (`src/`, `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs`, the engine copy, the vendored hub client).\n\n**What works (verified)**\n- `node tests/run.mjs` prints `KC TESTS: 54 passed, 0 failed`. It covers the data, technique timing and scoring, bouts, CPU balance and determinism, the three bonus rules, scene flow, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, and Dev mode.\n- The AI Hub harness passes all four configurations: 2 players, 4 players with latency/jitter/loss, host-leave, and the server role (13/13, 13/13, 13/13, 14/14).\n- Every scene draws without errors headless. Browser screenshots of the title, fight, and About page look right.\n- Playable pieces: title and menus, the Champon Road with all 17 nodes, lessons with autoplay, versus against the CPU, boards/pots/bull, records, About, Dev mode with live sliders and reset, the touch-only gate, sound effects, music, and gibberish referee voices.\n\n**What is missing or partial (these block the goal)**\n- Local Kachinuki for 3–4 players, and a second pad for versus. Player 2 is keyboard only.\n- The Technique Lab, filmstrips, and the 2.5D camera.\n- Attract mode runs exhibition bouts only, with no bonus reel, how-to card, or score loop.\n- The Xbox button setting doesn't change the prompts. Only PlayStation-style icons are drawn.\n- Online uses the hub's own card rather than a game card.\n- Stages have 1–3 props each rather than 3–8. Hit boxes are distance-based, not taken from the rig's joints.\n- Many planned suites are absent: input, UI, audio, the fake-hub online tests, and the drill and attract suites.\n- The AI handles jumpers poorly: Ebi (level 3) beats the level-10 autopilot. The playthrough therefore runs with the \"P1 invincible\" dev toggle on, which is a cheat, so it proves flow, not balance.\n- I only reviewed three screenshots, and no filmstrips.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- Title \"Karate Champon\" from the plan. File names use TASK.md's `claude-haiku-5-5-medium` form, not the plan's `opus-5-5-max`.\n- Online play is host-authoritative: the host runs the bouts and sends snapshots and events, and guests send only their own inputs.\n- The engine was used as shipped; I made no engine edits.\n- No subagents or workflows were used.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:43:15\n- Source: Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; Japanese title Karate Do)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified), AI Hub client 1.4.0, Web Audio; Node 24 and Python Playwright for tests\n- Art direction: engine Humanoid rigs as PS1-style pixel art at 320×240 with custom stage backdrops\n- Animation: engine rig driven from the fight state, with E.MOVES adapted for the karate techniques\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; local versus 1–2\n- Testing: headless suites (54 checks), a scripted playthrough, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 12 issues were caught. Four were breaking: the boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless store that kept no save, which stopped the story flow. All fixed; the suite is green.\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough.\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe, which mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`, which made scene checks possible.\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early; the store returns false with no storage.\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, CPU, bonus rules, drills, online Kachinuki, and the voice synth.\n- Hardest: getting the pose from fight state into the rig each step, while keeping that state serializable for guests.\n- Engine changes: none.\n- One thing to change: an in-memory store fallback for headless tests.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked: the staged structure and the explicit done-when list.\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names versus TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and the plan's scope, which one session can't finish.\n- Hardest: online netcode and the harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build and the expected test counts.","ttft_ms":1588,"type":"result","duration_ms":360200,"uuid":"1a79a4db-6498-4e21-b73b-b8b22fcba343","ttft_stream_ms":940,"time_to_request_ms":46,"first_content_frame_ms":940,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":3,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":28,"outputTokens":11016,"cacheReadInputTokens":5356922,"cacheCreationInputTokens":13501,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3089011,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":3323,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":13501,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}}}
