{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7q7afbDddbAAiYXkJQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The goal is still not fully met. I added the missing features the last report named, but some TASK.md items remain open (listed below), so I can't honestly say every feature is done.\n\n**Added this round**\n- **Prompts** follow the last input device. A gamepad shows Xbox letters when the engine's button-symbol setting is Xbox, and PlayStation shapes otherwise. Keyboard and mouse show keycaps.\n- **Attract mode** loops through an exhibition bout, a bonus reel, a how-to card, the high scores, and then the title. The test checks that sequence.\n- **Versus** has a keyboard second player, a fighter picker, and a stage picker.\n- **Local Kachinuki** runs 3 or 4 seats. The keyboard player is seat 0 and the rest are CPU rivals. The winner stays on, the loser goes to the back, and five bout wins makes a champion. Headless, a 3-seat run rotated the queue and produced a champion.\n- **Technique Lab** (Dev mode warp): pick a technique, set the gap to a dummy, and play it. It reports whether it connected, the gap, and the frame data.\n- **Tatami 2.5D** camera option, switching to the engine's brawler view in fights.\n- **Stages** draw five prop slots either side of the arena, including the stage's own props.\n- **Dev toggles** for hit boxes, frame data, and input display now draw. Before, they were stored but did nothing.\n- **Touch-only gate** is evaluated on the title and attract screens, and lifts on the first keyboard or pad input.\n- **Referee calls** are spoken with the gibberish voice.\n- **Online**: the AI Hub harness passes all four configurations (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter, and loss; host-leave; server role). The 2-player check was re-run after the latest changes.\n\n**Current state**\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 59 passed, 0 failed.\n- Bundle: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315.html` in the work folder, no external requests.\n- `exit-interview.json` parses, and its answers match `data/about.json`.\n- No TODO, FIXME, or placeholder text in `src/`.\n\n**Still open against TASK.md**\n- Local multiplayer for 3–4 humans. Only the keyboard player is human; the rest of the seats are CPU.\n- Pad-based second player in versus. Player 2 is keyboard only.\n- Online play uses the hub's own card, not a card the game draws itself.\n- Joint-based hit boxes. Hits use distance and height rules instead.\n- Several planned suites are not written: input, UI, audio, and a fake-hub online suite.\n- Balance: Ebi beats the level-10 autopilot. The scripted playthrough therefore uses the \"P1 invincible\" dev toggle, so it proves the flow but not balance.\n- Limited art and review: I looked at only three screenshots and no filmstrips, and stages still have simple art.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:43:15\n- Source: Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; Japanese title Karate Do)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view, with an optional 2.5D tatami view\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified), AI Hub client 1.4.0 (unmodified), Web Audio; Node 24 and Python Playwright for tests\n- Art direction: engine Humanoid rigs as PS1-style pixel art at 320×240, with custom stage backdrops\n- Animation: engine rig driven from the fight state, with E.MOVES adapted for the karate techniques\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; local versus 1–2 players; local Kachinuki 3–4 seats with CPU rivals\n- Testing: 59 headless checks, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness (4 configurations)\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 12 issues were caught early, and four were breaking: a boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless save that kept nothing. All four are fixed.\n- Later, the hub harness and the modes suite caught my own parser error and an attract-loop assumption. Both are fixed.\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough, which crossed every scene transition at once.\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe. It mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`, which made scene checks possible.\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early. The store returns false with no storage, so I added a memory fallback in the game.\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, the CPU, the bonus rules, the drills, the online Kachinuki, the Technique Lab, and the voice synth.\n- Hardest: pushing the fight state into the rig each step without drawing on every frame, while keeping that state serializable for online guests.\n- Engine changes: none.\n- One change I'd want: an in-memory store fallback so headless tests can use the same save path.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked: the staged structure (read STAGE.md, with TASK.md winning over PLAN.md) and the explicit done-when list.\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names against TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and a plan scope that one session can't finish.\n- Hardest: the online netcode and the harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build, and the expected test counts."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":533,"cache_read_input_tokens":456965,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":533},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772","uuid":"6ee145f2-6b6e-4044-b916-13900b909f76","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:43:21.202Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh7q7Jnfw6MybKd3HAW5"}
