{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh83qgoKaBZHFkq2PGmU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've closed the two checkable gaps from the last round. The one I can't close here is testing the pad path on real controllers. So the feature requirement is still not fully verified, and I'm not claiming the goal is met.\n\n**Closed this round**\n- **Hub configurations on the final build:** all four pass. Two players: 14/14. Four players with latency, jitter and loss: 14/14. Host-leave: 14/14. Server role: 15/15.\n- **The game's own online card:** the game now draws the card instead of the hub's. It shows the players in their seat colours, who is ready, the win goal, and the countdown. Enter or cross answers Ready, and triangle asks for more time. The hub's Enter-answers-Ready check passes.\n\n**Still open**\n- **Real controllers:** the pad path is only tested with a stubbed gamepad. The four-human local setup (keyboard, keyboard, two pads) hasn't been tried on real hardware.\n- **Joint-based hit boxes:** the plan asks for them, but TASK.md doesn't. Hits use distance and height rules instead.\n- **Test suites from the plan:** I wrote part of the planned set, but there's no UI suite and no separate input suite.\n- **Balance:** Ebi (level 3) still beats the level-10 autopilot, so the scripted playthrough uses the P1-invincible dev toggle.\n\n**Files and checks**\n- Bundle: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315.html` in the work folder. It is one file with no external requests.\n- `exit-interview.json` in the same folder. It parses and has all five top-level sections.\n- `node tests/run.mjs`: 66 passed, 0 failed.\n- No TODO, FIXME, or placeholder text in `src/`.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:43:15\n- Source: Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; Japanese title Karate Do)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view, with an optional 2.5D tatami view\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified), AI Hub client 1.4.0 (unmodified), Web Audio; Node 24 and Python Playwright for tests\n- Art direction: engine Humanoid rigs as PS1-style pixel art at 320×240, with custom stage backdrops\n- Animation: engine rig driven from the fight state, with E.MOVES adapted for the karate techniques\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; the game draws its own card; local versus 1–2 players; local Kachinuki with up to 4 humans\n- Testing: 66 headless checks, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness (4 configurations)\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 12 issues were caught early, four of them breaking: a boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless save that kept nothing. All four are fixed.\n- Later, the hub harness and the modes suite caught my own parser error and an attract-loop assumption. Both are fixed.\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough, which crossed every scene transition at once.\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe, which mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`, which made scene checks possible.\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early. The store returns false with no storage, so I added a memory fallback in the game.\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, the CPU, the bonus rules, the drills, the online Kachinuki, the Technique Lab, the pad input, and the voice synth.\n- Hardest: pushing the fight state into the rig each step without drawing on every frame, while keeping that state serializable for online guests.\n- Engine changes: none.\n- One change I'd want: an in-memory store fallback so headless tests can use the same save path.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked: the staged structure (read STAGE.md, with TASK.md winning over PLAN.md) and the explicit done-when list.\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names against TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and a plan scope that one session can't finish.\n- Hardest: the online netcode and the harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build, and the expected test counts."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1144,"cache_read_input_tokens":476813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1144},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772","uuid":"ee1ed84d-2fab-40dc-aa39-159701b6a95c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:46:11.102Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh83qNxFNHurHsRV19s7"}
