he karate techniques\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; the game draws its own card; local versus 1–2 players; local Kachinuki with up to 4 humans\n- Testing: 78 headless checks, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness (4 configurations)\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 12 issues were caught early, four of them breaking: a boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless save that kept nothing. All four are fixed.\n- Later checks caught my own mistakes: a parser error, an attract-loop assumption, a wrong matrix count, and a placeholder check that always passed. All are fixed.\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough.\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe, which mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`.\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early. The store returns false with no storage, so I added a memory fallback.\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, CPU, bonus rules, drills, online Kachinuki, Technique Lab, pad input, and voice synth.\n- Hardest: pushing fight state into the rig each step without drawing on every frame, and keeping that state serializable for online guests.\n- Engine changes: none.\n- One change I'd want: an in-memory store fallback so headless tests use the same save path.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked: the staged structure (read STAGE.md, TASK.md wins over PLAN.md) and the explicit done-when list.\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names against TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and a plan scope one session can't finish.\n- Hardest: the online netcode and harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build, and say whether real-controller testing is expected when no hardware is available."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1975,"cache_read_input_tokens":487366,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1975},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772","uuid":"a2b16534-3de7-4695-8471-1f45ce9ad124","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:47:06.676Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh87tsfMaTbCi8M7S7xM"}
